# Environments — .venv is ~950 MB. Never commit it.
.venv/
venv/
env/
.python-version

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Results are regenerated by the runner and are gitignored per spec §8.
# Results: bulk run output is regenerable and stays out of git.
# Git cannot un-ignore a file inside an ignored DIRECTORY, so this
# ignores the contents rather than the directory itself — otherwise
# the negations below silently do nothing and the figures never ship.
results/*
!results/figures/
results/figures/*

# EXCEPTION — the E2 grid and its four shards ARE committed. 523 KB total.
#
# Not because they are precious, but because five scripts and three fidelity
# gates anchor to `results/e2-grid.json` BY PATH. While it was ignored, A's clone
# and B's clone held two different E2 runs under that one filename, and every
# gate passed in both — a gate that compares a regeneration against an untracked
# file can only report that a clone agrees with itself. That is how a -0.0708
# from B's machine ended up in the record as an independent recomputation of A's
# -0.0595. The shards are committed too, so `run_e2.py --merge` is auditable
# rather than a claim in a log line. See tests/test_e2_provenance.py.
!results/e2-grid.json
!results/e2-grid-d6-s0.25.json
!results/e2-grid-d6-s0.1.json
!results/e2-grid-d8-s0.25.json
!results/e2-grid-d8-s0.1.json

# EXCEPTION — Q52 §2's budget-to-target grid, for the same reason and one more.
#
# docs/RESULTS.md anchors its entire Q52 §2 entry to this file BY PATH, and this
# project's own Rule 1 is that a number with no committed file does not go in. It is
# also the only artefact in results/ that carries a `provenance` block — git sha,
# timestamp, argv, library versions, full config — so committing it is what makes the
# grid re-runnable rather than remembered. 136 KB.
#
# Noted because it nearly went the other way: `results/*` ignores .json by default and
# only `*.md` and `*.log` are negated below, so this file was silently unstageable
# after the run finished. That is the aa0785d failure mode exactly — a blanket rule
# meaning an artefact "never ships" while every log line claims it was written.
!results/q52-budget-to-target.json

# EXCEPTION — the D20 rescore. It is the artefact behind a CORRECTION to published
# numbers (Q35's "best observed" column and therefore L6, and the DoE arm's rule A on
# every external family), so it has to be auditable by anyone who reads the corrected
# claim. It also carries its own fidelity gate: worst |delta| = 0.0 over 400 Q42 rows
# and 200 Q35 rows on the columns the fix does not touch.
!results/d20-rescore.json

# D17, and it is the SAME defect one more time. `results/e2-doe-d8.json` was
# recorded as "does not exist", which is true of the repository and false of the
# disk: `run_e2_doe_d8.py:257` wrote it, `results/*` above ignored it, and nobody
# force-added it. It holds both d=8 cells, 50 rows each, 18 fields, and it
# regenerates the log text exactly — 0.0948 at sigma=0.10 and 0.0963 at 0.25.
# So the Q27 primary contrast IS re-aggregatable, contrary to the D17 entry.
# Committed here for the same reason as the grid: it is anchored by path.
!results/e2-doe-d8.json

# Q50's per-seed shards, committed on the grid's rationale — `--merge` reads them
# by path, so an uncommitted shard makes the merge unauditable. g00-g07 were
# force-added without a rule, which silently left g08-g19 behind when the run was
# completed; naming the pattern is what stops that recurring.
!results/q50-qlogei-g[0-9][0-9].json
!results/q50-qlogei-seedsweep.json

# ESTIMAND-EVALUATION.md quotes these numbers, so by that document's own §3.0
# argument they have to be in the repository rather than in one clone.
!results/estimand-evaluation.json

# The paired contrast CLAIMS.md leads with. Committed for a reason the D12/D17 pattern
# has now earned: this file holds the per-INSTANCE vectors for both arms, so the next
# person can re-pair without re-running anything, and `tests/test_q50_paired.py` can
# check the docs against an artefact instead of against a memory. It is also the only
# record that the published figures were once computed over 8 seeds rather than 20.
!results/q50-paired.json

# Go/no-go on whether the in-house coating data can carry a Phase 3 figure. Committed
# because the answer is NO and that is a decision the project should not have to
# rediscover -- the reasoning is in the file, not just in a chat log.
!results/lab-phase3-feasibility.json

# Oracle instances: DECIDED — the version-stamped ensembles under
# data/oracles/<oracle_version>/ ARE committed, parquet included. 456 KB total.
#
# Why, given they are nominally regenerable from a seed: the acceptance loop is
# rejection sampling wrapped around scipy's L-BFGS-B, and `oracle_version` hashes the
# construction parameters, NOT the optimiser's behaviour. Two machines on different
# scipy builds can therefore accept different instances from the same seed under the
# same version string, and nothing downstream would notice — which is precisely the
# silent-mixing failure the version field exists to prevent, displaced from the config
# to the platform. Committing the artefact makes the ensemble authoritative and
# scripts/generate_oracles.py the audit trail. See test_the_committed_ensemble_loads.
#
# Loose dumps at the top level are still junk and stay ignored.
data/oracles/*.parquet
data/oracles/*.pt

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/

# scratch
COMMIT_MSG.txt
!results/*.md
!results/*.log
!results/figures/*.png

# Q53 shards and the merged grid. Anchored by path from docs/RESULTS.md Q53, and the
# merged file is what the generality claim rests on. 5 design draws per seed means these
# are not regenerable in a coffee break: the grid took ~2.3 h of wall clock.
!results/q53-spread-gp-families.json
!results/q53-spread-gp-*.json
!results/q53-fidelity-gate.json
